\fs24 \cf0 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1 \cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
JSX stands for 
\f2\b JavaScript XML
\f1\b0 . It is not required by React, but it is a syntactic extension that makes writing UI components significantly more readable.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 core question:\
If we use 
\f3\fs26 .js
\f1\fs24  in React, isn\'92t it the same thing?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs28 \cf0 Short Answer:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Yes, it works.\uc0\u8232 But if you are writing JSX, you are not writing pure JavaScript \'97 you are writing syntax that must be transpiled.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 What Is JSX?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 JSX allows you to write HTML-like syntax inside JavaScript:
\f3\fs26 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 const element = <h1>Hello World</h1>;\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 This is 
\f2\b not valid plain JavaScript
\f1\b0 . Browsers cannot interpret it directly.\
A transpiler like Babel converts it into:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 const element = React.createElement("h1", null, "Hello World"); \'97> For pure JS\
\
So JSX is essentially 
\f2\b syntactic sugar
\f1\b0  over 
\f3\fs26 React.createElement()
\f1\fs24  calls.\
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 In modern React setups (Vite, Create React App, Next.js), you can write JSX inside 
\f3\fs26 .js
\f1\fs24  files without any issue because the build system is configured to transpile them.\
For example, this works perfectly in 
\f3\fs26 App.js
\f1\fs24 :\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 function App() \{\
return <div>Hello</div>;\
\}\
\
The file extension does not change how JSX works \'97 it is just a convention.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 Why Does 
\f4\fs39 .jsx
\f2\fs36  Exist?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 Primarily for:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Semantic clarity\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Readability in large codebases\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Explicitly signaling that the file contains JSX\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 It is a convention, not a technical requirement.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Can You Write React Without JSX?\
\
Yes. This is functionally identical to using JSX. \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 However:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It is harder to read\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It becomes verbose for nested structures\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
It reduces maintainability\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 JSX dramatically improves developer ergonomics.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 const element = React.createElement(\
"h1",\
null,\
"Hello"\
);\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 Important Technical Detail\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 JSX is:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Not HTML\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Not a string\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Not a template literal\
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Not interpreted at runtime\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 It is compiled at build time into JavaScript function calls.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 For example:\
\
const x = <div />;\
\
This produces a JavaScript object (a React element), not a DOM node.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs36 \cf0 Summary\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0
\f3\b0\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
.jsx
\f1\fs24  is only a file extension convention.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
You can write JSX inside 
\f3\fs26 .js
\f1\fs24  files.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
JSX is transpiled into 
\f3\fs26 React.createElement()
\f1\fs24  calls.\
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
JSX is optional, but practically standard in modern React development.\
\pard\tx566\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
NOTE : 
\f3\fs26 className
\f1\fs24  is used instead of 
\f3\fs26 class\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs24 \cf0 This JSX:\
<div className="container" />\
\
This JS: \
React.createElement("div", \{ className: "container" \});\
\
\

